NNAAMMEE
     oodd — octal, decimal, hex, ascii dump

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     oodd [--aaBBbbccDDddeeFFffHHhhIIiiLLllOOoossvvXXxx] [--AA _b_a_s_e] [--jj _o_f_f_s_e_t] [--NN _l_e_n_g_t_h]
        [--tt _t_y_p_e___s_t_r_i_n_g] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The oodd utility is a filter which displays the specified files, or stan‐
     dard input if no files are specified, in a user specified format.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA _b_a_s_e
             Specify the input address base.  The argument _b_a_s_e may be one of
             dd, oo, xx, or nn, which specify decimal, octal, hexadecimal ad‐
             dresses or no address, respectively.

     --aa      _O_n_e_-_b_y_t_e _c_h_a_r_a_c_t_e_r _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal,
             followed by sixteen space-separated, three column, space-filled,
             characters of input data per line.  Control characters are
             printed as their names instead of as C-style escapes.

     --BB      Same as --oo.

     --bb      _O_n_e_-_b_y_t_e _o_c_t_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by sixteen space-separated, three column, zero-filled,
             bytes of input data, in octal, per line.  This is the default
             output style if no other is selected.

     --cc      _O_n_e_-_b_y_t_e _c_h_a_r_a_c_t_e_r _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal,
             followed by sixteen space-separated, three column, space-filled,
             characters of input data per line.  Control characters are
             printed as c style escapes, or as three octal digits, if no c es‐
             cape exists for the character.

     --DD      _F_o_u_r_-_b_y_t_e _o_c_t_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by four space-separated, ten column, space filled, four-
             byte units of input data, in octal, per line.

     --dd      _T_w_o_-_b_y_t_e _u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d _d_e_c_i_m_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in
             octal, followed by eight space-separated, five column, zero-
             filled, two-byte units of input data, in unsigned decimal, per
             line.

     --ee      _E_i_g_h_t_-_b_y_t_e _f_l_o_a_t_i_n_g _p_o_i_n_t _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in
             octal, followed by two space-separated, twenty-one column, space
             filled, eight-byte units of input data, in floating point, per
             line.

     --FF      Same as --ee.

     --ff      _F_o_u_r_-_b_y_t_e _f_l_o_a_t_i_n_g _p_o_i_n_t _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in
             octal, followed by four space-separated, 14 column, space filled,
             four-byte units of input data, in floating point, per line.

     --HH      _F_o_u_r_-_b_y_t_e _h_e_x _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by four space-separated, eight column, zero filled, four-
             byte units of input data, in hex, per line.

     --hh      _T_w_o_-_b_y_t_e _h_e_x _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by eight space-separated, four column, zero filled, two-
             byte units of input data, in hex, per line.

     --II      _F_o_u_r_-_b_y_t_e _d_e_c_i_m_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal,
             followed by four space-separated, eleven column, space filled,
             four-byte units of input data, in decimal, per line.

     --ii      _T_w_o_-_b_y_t_e _d_e_c_i_m_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal,
             followed by eight space-separated, six column, space filled, two-
             byte units of input data, in decimal, per line.

     --jj _o_f_f_s_e_t
             Skip _o_f_f_s_e_t bytes from the beginning of the input.  By default,
             _o_f_f_s_e_t is interpreted as a decimal number.  With a leading 00xx or
             00XX, _o_f_f_s_e_t is interpreted as a hexadecimal number, otherwise,
             with a leading 00, _o_f_f_s_e_t is interpreted as an octal number.  Ap‐
             pending the character bb, kk, or mm to _o_f_f_s_e_t causes it to be inter‐
             preted as a multiple of 512, 1024, or 1048576, respectively.

             As an alternative to oodd --jj _o_f_f_s_e_t [_f_i_l_e], the X/Open System In‐
             terfaces syntax oodd [_f_i_l_e] [++]_o_f_f_s_e_t[..][BBbb] is also supported.

     --LL      Same as --II.

     --ll      Same as --II.

     --NN _l_e_n_g_t_h
             Interpret only _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes of input.

     --OO      _F_o_u_r_-_b_y_t_e _o_c_t_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by four space-separated, eleven column, zero-filled, four-
             byte units of input data, in octal, per line.

     --oo      _T_w_o_-_b_y_t_e _o_c_t_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in octal, fol‐
             lowed by eight space-separated, six column, zero-filled, two-byte
             units of input data, in octal, per line.

     --ss      _T_w_o_-_b_y_t_e _s_i_g_n_e_d _d_e_c_i_m_a_l _d_i_s_p_l_a_y.  Display the input offset in oc‐
             tal, followed by eight space-separated, five column, zero-filled,
             two-byte units of input data, in signed decimal, per line.

     --tt _t_y_p_e___s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify one or more output types.  The _t_y_p_e___s_t_r_i_n_g option-argu‐
             ment must be a string specifying the types to be used when writ‐
             ing the input data.  The string must consist of the type specifi‐
             cation characters:

             aa selects US-ASCII output, with control characters replaced with
             their names instead of as c escape sequences.  See also the __uu
             conversion provided by hexdump(1).

             cc selects a standard character based conversion.  See also the __cc
             conversion provided by hexdump(1).

             ff selects the floating point output format.  This type character
             can be optionally followed by the characters 44 or FF to specify
             four-byte floating point output, or 88 or LL to specify eight-byte
             floating point output.  The default output format is eight-byte
             floats.  See also the ee conversion provided by hexdump(1).

             dd, oo, uu, or xx select decimal, octal, unsigned decimal, or hex
             output respectively.  These types can optionally be followed by CC
             to specify _c_h_a_r-sized output, SS to specify _s_h_o_r_t-sized output, II
             to specify _i_n_t-sized output, LL to specify _l_o_n_g-sized output, 11 to
             specify one-byte output, 22 to specify two-byte output, 44 to spec‐
             ify four-byte output, or 88 to specify eight-byte output.  The de‐
             fault output format is in four-byte quantities.  See also the dd,
             oo, uu, and xx conversions provided by hexdump(1).

     --vv      The --vv option causes oodd to display all input data.  Without the
             --vv option, any number of groups of output lines, which would be
             identical to the immediately preceding group of output lines (ex‐
             cept for the input offsets), are replaced with a line comprised
             of a single asterisk.

     --XX      Same as --HH.

     --xx      Same as --hh.

     For each input file, oodd sequentially copies the input to standard output,
     transforming the data according to the options given.  If no options are
     specified, the default display is equivalent to specifying the --oo option.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The oodd utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
